NAME
       podman-build - build a container image from a Containerfile

SYNOPSIS
       podman build [-t NAME[:TAG]] [-f FILE] [--no-cache] [-q] [DIRECTORY]

DESCRIPTION
       podman build reads a Containerfile (or Dockerfile) and builds an image
       from it, step by step. DIRECTORY (the current directory when not
       given) is the build context: the files that COPY and ADD can use. The
       file is looked for there as Containerfile, then Dockerfile, unless -f
       names it.

       Each instruction is a line starting with a keyword:

       FROM IMAGE        the image to start from (first line)
       RUN COMMAND       run a command inside, such as dnf -y install httpd
       COPY SRC DEST     copy files from the context into the image
       ADD SRC DEST      like COPY, also for URLs and archives
       ENV NAME=VALUE    set an environment variable
       EXPOSE PORT       record a port the program listens on
       USER NAME|UID     the user later steps and the container run as
       WORKDIR DIR       the working directory
       LABEL KEY=VALUE   add metadata
       CMD ["prog","arg"]         the default command
       ENTRYPOINT ["prog"]        the fixed program; CMD gives its arguments

       The new image is stored locally under the name given with -t, with
       localhost/ in front when the name has no registry (localhost/myweb).

OPTIONS
       -t, --tag NAME[:TAG]
              Name the image.

       -f, --file FILE
              The Containerfile to use.

       --no-cache
              Do not reuse the results of earlier builds.

       -q, --quiet
              Print only the new image's ID.

EXAMPLES
       Build an image from the Containerfile in ~/build and run it:

           cd ~/build
           podman build -t myweb:1.0 .
           podman images
           podman run -d --name myweb -p 8080:8080 localhost/myweb:1.0

       A small Containerfile:

           FROM registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/httpd-24
           COPY index.html /var/www/html/
           EXPOSE 8080

SEE ALSO
       podman(1), podman-run(1), buildah(1)
